The Supercollider plugin interface uses a class `SCFFT_Allocator` in the interface methods `fSCfftCreate` and `fSCfftDestroy`.

Unfortunately, `SCFFT_Allocator` is an abstract base class and calling virtual methods across plugin boundaries should be avoided (unless you use COM techniques). Different compilers can have different vtable layouts and your plugin may fail/crash in mysterious ways because the wrong virtual method is getting called. Practical example: recent GCC uses two (!) virtual methods for the virtual destructor while MSVC uses one, this means that subsequent virtual methods will be offset by 1.

The allocator should be rather something like this (similar to `FifoMsg`):

```
typedef void *(*AllocFunc)(void * user, size_t size);
typedef void (*AsyncStageFn)(void * user, void *ptr);

class SCFFT_Allocator {
    void *mUser;
    AllocFunc mAlloc;
    FreeFunc mFree;
public:
    // the constructor makes sure that old user-defined subclasses don't compile
    SCFFT_Allocator(void *user, AllocFunc allocFn, FreeFunc freeFn)
        : mUser(user), mAlloc(allocFn), mFree(freeFn) {}
    void *alloc(size_t size) {
        mAlloc(mUser, size);
    }
    void free(void *ptr) {
        mFree(mUser, ptr);
    }
};

class SCWorld_Allocator : public SCFFT_Allocator {
public:
    SCWorld_Allocator(InterfaceTable* ft, World* world)
        // casting function pointers and calling them without casting back to the original type
        // is technically UB, but in this case it works because World * and void * have the same size
        : SCFFT_Allocator((void *)world), (AllocFunc)(ft->fRTAlloc), (FreeFunc)(ft->fRTFree)) {}
};
```

This could be done for the next time the plugin API version changes. UGens using `SCWorld_Allocator` just have to recompiled, but if they use their own subclass of `SCFFT_Allocator `(unlikely), they must change some code.

