Hello,
I came across a surprising crash with supernova and I traced it down to running multiple SendTrig UGens in a Parallel Group. I'm on the recent develop build on macOS 10.12.
The following will crash on my machine after few triggers of SendTrig (I assume this indeterminacy is because the synths are started asynchronously). I would really appreciate if others confirmed this happening on different systems as well. Reproducer:

```
Server.supernova;
s.boot;

(
fork{
	~mainGroup = ParGroup.new;
	// ~mainGroup = Group.new; //doesn't crash in a regular group
	s.sync;
	~def = OSCdef(\update, {|msg| postf("id: %, time: %\n", msg[2], msg[3].round(0.01))}, '/tr'); //post triggers to see progress
	~trigSynths = 4.collect({|doInc| //crash happens for me with 3 and more SendTrig synths
		{SendTrig.kr(Impulse.kr(4), doInc, Sweep.kr)}.play(target: ~mainGroup); //the faster the impulse, the sooner the crash happens
	});
}
)

~trigSynths.do(_.free); ~def.free;
```

## Environment

- SuperCollider version: 3.10.3
- Operating system: Ubuntu Studio 18.04

Based on a test (pasted below), we find that TempoClock in Linux drifts away from the server's sample output to a degree not observed in Mac/Windows.

## Methodology

The source signal consists of easily recognizable attacks, with silence in between. The triggers are generated in one of two ways:

- `\routine` (experimental group) -- a client-side routine sends `factor` messages per second, equally spaced in time. These are time stamped, and the synth uses `OffsetOut` so that the audio is not quantized to control block boundaries.

- `\impulse` (control group) -- triggers are generated by `Impulse.ar(factor)`.

This signal is recorded into a buffer and retrieved into the client. Then we scan for the exact sample index of each impulse, and differentiate to obtain inter-onset intervals (IOIs). Then we can report the minimum, maximum, mean and median IOI as well as the total time.

## Findings

In all tests, the control group always gets IOIs = `sample rate / factor`. This is the ideal result (not possible using a routine in real-time), demonstrating that the measurement/analysis code is correct.

In Mac/Windows (using `\routine`), another user observed deviation away from the ideal of +/- 2 or 3 samples, with the average being very close to the control result and the median matching the control. This is acceptable IMO.

In Linux (using `\routine`), I observe IOIs ranging ~~+2 to +60 samples~~ up to 6% longer than expected. This is bad for two reasons:

- Jitter is rather high.
- Also, the deviation is sharply biased positive, meaning that all durations are longer than expected. So there is a cumulative drift. (In another conversation, there was the question "the time interval between bundles being processed by the server is greater than factor.reciprocal. Isn't that expected behavior?" No, that's not expected. You might expect all *absolute times* to be late, but IOIs are the first-order derivative and should absorb a consistent, global shift. If all time intervals are longer than expected, then it means that everything is running slow, and you will cumulatively drift later and later. If the problem is randomly distributed jitter, then you would find some IOIs larger than the correct value and other IOIs shorter. That isn't the pattern I observe.)

```
~clockTest.(\impulse, 100, 2);  // 100 impulses per second, for about 2 seconds
[ 441, 441, 441.0, 441 ]
1.89 seconds between first and last, expected 1.89

~clockTest.(\routine, 100, 2);  // 100 impulses per second, for about 2 seconds
[ 446, 494, 459.24338624339, 458 ]
1.9681859410431 seconds between first and last, expected 1.89

~clockTest.(\routine, 100, 10);  // 100 impulses per second, for about 10 seconds
[ 430, 612, 458.38777660695, 457 ]
9.8641723356009 seconds between first and last, expected 9.49

// ^^ so, the longer you run it, the more drift accumulates

~clockTest.(\routine, 10, 10);  // 10 impulses per second, for about 10 seconds
[ 4418, 4672, 4462.8404255319, 4441.0 ]
9.5126303854875 seconds between first and last, expected 9.4

// ^^ but the drift is significantly less when you reduce the event rate
```

## Expected vs actual

Expected behavior: Linux's clock accuracy should be reasonably close to the Mac/Windows observations.

Actual behavior: The large deviation, and cumulative deviation, is enough to break some use cases in Linux which are fine in other OSes (e.g., high density granular synthesis under control of patterns).

## Trying to make sense of it

For each routine iteration, TempoClock wakes up and sets its logical time to a number of beats, which is associated with an absolute number of seconds. This number of seconds is added to server messaging latency and converted to an OSC timestamp. Then, the server is supposed to interpret the timestamp based on the same timebase, and schedule the audio to hit the speakers at the same time.

AFAICS both sclang and scsynth are using `std::chrono::high_resolution_clock`. Intuitively, I would expect, if sclang's IOI is supposed to be 10 ms, then the timestamps should be 10 ms apart, and scsynth should read that as the same 10 ms (based on the same `std::chrono::high_resolution_clock`).

We do see this in Mac/Windows. We don't see this in Linux. (But, `TempoClock::Run()` contains no platform-specific #ifdefs.)

Some drift is expected because the physical sample rate (coming from the soundcard's clock) does not exactly match the system. I usually see ~44095 samples per second. If this explained the drift in Linux, I would expect a/ significantly less drift and b/ slightly negative drift (where I observe noticeably positive drift).

The variables at play are:

- sclang's logical time in seconds
- scsynth's timebase for seconds (to interpret the OSC timestamp)
- OSC messaging jitter (which should be absorbed by the 200 ms messaging latency -- the data retrieved support this reading, so I think we can rule that out)

The fact that our code is the same across platforms, but we observe different results in different platforms, suggests that the supporting high-resolution clock behaves differently in different OSes.

But, we have two processes using the same clock. I can't explain how the two processes proceed at different speeds.

## Test case

This doesn't follow the typical "minimal example" format because we have to collect data and analyze it, and that takes extra code. So I structured the test to define a `~clockTest` function, which you then invoke with one line.

```
~clockTest.(trigStyle, factor, bufdur);

~clockTest.(\impulse, 100, 2);  // 100 impulses per second, for about 2 seconds
~clockTest.(\routine, 100, 2);
```

```
(
~clockTest = { |trigStyle = \routine, factor = 100, bufdur = 2|  // also \impulse
	// uses interpreter variables, not safe to run concurrently
	if(b.isKindOf(Buffer) and: { b.numFrames.notNil }) {
		Error("Another test is active. Wait for it to finish.").throw;
	};
	s.waitForBoot {
		var recSynth;
		b = Buffer.alloc(s, s.sampleRate * bufdur, 1);
		SynthDef("help-OffsetOut", { arg out = 0, freq = 440, dur = 0.005;
			var env, trig, count, num;
			if(trigStyle == \routine) {
				env = EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.0001, dur, 0.2), doneAction:2);
			} {
				trig = Impulse.ar(factor);
				count = PulseCount.ar(trig);
				num = b.duration * factor * 0.95;
				FreeSelf.kr(count >= num);
				env = EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.0001, dur, 0.2), trig);
			};
			OffsetOut.ar(out, SinOsc.ar(freq, 0, env))
		}).send(s);
		SynthDef(\rec, { |out, bufnum, bus|
			var rec, done;
			rec = RecordBuf.ar(In.ar(bus, 1), bufnum, loop: 0, doneAction: 2);
			Line.kr(0, 1, b.duration, doneAction: 2);  // FFS
		}).send(s);
		s.sync;

		s.makeBundle(0.2, {
			recSynth = Synth(\rec, [bufnum: b, bus: 0]);
		});

		if(trigStyle == \routine) {
			(b.duration * factor * 0.95).do {
				s.sendBundle(0.2, ["/s_new", "help-OffsetOut", -1, 2, recSynth.nodeID]);
				factor.reciprocal.wait;
			};
		} {
			s.sendBundle(0.2, ["/s_new", "help-OffsetOut", -1, 2, recSynth.nodeID]);
			b.duration.wait;
		};

		// exited loop, recording should be done
		b.getToFloatArray(0, b.numFrames, wait: -1, action: { |data| d = data });
		// 'forkIfNeeded' in that method, should be all finished
		// scan for onsets
		o = List.new;
		(2 .. d.size-1).do { |i|
			if(d[i-2] == 0 and: { d[i-1] == 0 and: { d[i] != 0 } }) {
				o.add(i);
			}
		};

		p = o.differentiate.drop(1);
		[p.minItem, p.maxItem, p.mean, p.median].postln;
		"% seconds between first and last, expected %\n".postf(
			(o.maxItem - o.minItem) / s.sampleRate,
			(o.size - 1) / factor
		);

		b.free;
	};
};
)
```

## Environment

* SuperCollider version: 3.10.3
* Operating system: Ubuntu Studio 18.04

## Steps to reproduce

https://www.listarc.bham.ac.uk/lists/sc-users/msg65580.html

Evidence from this thread strongly suggests that std::chrono::high_resolution_clock does not exhibit accurate timing in Linux, and that it is significantly less inaccurate in Mac and Windows.

The inaccuracy appears to be proportional to the number of times per second that the clock is asked to awake. If I run a 12 second test with 10 events per second, sclang loses about 0.145 seconds (about 10 ms deviation per second, or 1% slower). If I run the same test with 100 events per second, sclang loses about 0.480 seconds (about 40 ms/sec, or 4% slower).

So the effect is minimal for typical musical contexts, where 20-30 events per second would cover most cases. But it does mean that high-density granular synthesis with one grain per synth is not reliable in Linux presently.

Also, the effect is cumulative: the total drag is worse for a 12 second test compared to a 2 second test.

If the effect were networking jitter, I would expect some of the measured times to be shorter than expected and others to be longer, with the average being close to correct. But all of the measurements skew long. The only explanation that makes any sense is that TempoClock, in Linux, runs slower than real time. TempoClock relies on std::chrono::high_resolution_clock, leading to the conclusion that std::chrono does not work well in Linux.

